CORE
main.c
--unwind 2 --rounds 4 --por --allow-pointer-unsoundness --array-rf
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
--
`a[i] = v` is `a#2 = with(a#1, i, v)` in the SSA, so an array write is a
read-modify-write of the WHOLE array and the lazy chain has to carry every
element once per (write, round). Array objects are 64-90% of the formula across
the data-race family for that reason, and neither arrays-uf=auto nor
--narrow-shared reaches it: the first keeps the array-valued terms, the second
slices byte ranges and an array has no fixed width.

--array-rf answers an element read by matching the writes instead --
ite(exec(w) && i_w == j, val_w, ... , default) over the writes before it in
schedule order -- so the cost stops depending on the array's size. A cost model
decides per object. The flag is on by default now; this test still names it
explicitly, so that the test keeps testing the same thing if the default moves
again.

The array is 1000 elements on purpose: the cost model leaves a small array on
the chain, and the test would then prove nothing.

This is the under-constraining direction: a[idx] can only hold 0, 1 or 2, so a
read left free to invent a value makes this safe program report a violation it
does not have. Narrowing is visible here too -- 59933 clauses on the chain,
26823 with --array-rf.
